course: dictionary
lesson: 4052
title: Generator
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A generator is a machine that makes electricity. Its name comes from the word "generate", which means to make or produce something. Huge generators sit in power stations, and small ones can be carried to a camp.
explain: The word can also mean anything that produces something. «An idea generator» is something that makes ideas, and «a random number generator» picks numbers by chance. To choose the right meaning, read the words around it: is the sentence about electricity or about making things?
explain: «In a power cut, use a torch, not a candle, keep the fridge closed, and never touch a fallen line; hospitals have generators that start automatically.» The clue is "power cut": when the electricity stops, something must make it again. So a generator is a machine that makes electricity.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in: "hospitals have machines that make electricity that start automatically" still makes sense, so the meaning fits.
fact: A generator is a machine that makes electricity.
fact: «Generator» can also mean anything that produces something, such as «an idea generator».
fact: Almost all electricity comes from something spinning a generator; solar panels are the exception.
fact: A «backup generator» is a spare machine that makes electricity when the normal power fails.
fact: A «random number generator» picks numbers by chance, and is used in maths and computing.
fact: Beware of sites offering «free Robux» or «V-Bucks generators»: they are scams that steal accounts or install harmful software.
exercise: «The wind pushes its long blades round, and a generator in the box at the top makes electricity.» What makes the electricity? | The long blades alone | The wind on its own, with no machine | A torch | *The generator
exercise: «Hospitals have generators that start automatically.» Find and copy one word that names a machine. | automatically | hospitals | *generators | start
exercise: Ahmed says a random number generator is a machine for cooking. Is he right? | *No, it picks numbers by chance, in maths and computing | Yes, it makes electricity for the kitchen | Yes, it cooks numbers | No, it is a kind of fridge
test: «In a power cut, the fridge must stay closed.» What does «power cut» mean here? | When the lights get brighter | *When the electricity stops | A cut in a piece of cloth | A sharp knife for cutting power cables
test: Which phrase means a spare machine that makes electricity when the main power fails? | A random number generator | A power line | *A backup generator | An idea generator
exam: «Almost all electricity comes from something spinning a generator; solar panels are the exception.» What does generator mean here? | Anything that stops electricity | *A machine that makes electricity by spinning, which is why almost all power comes from one | A machine that makes electricity | A sort of solar panel
